// Equipment library for popular all-in-one (single vessel) electric brewing systems.
//
// Figures marked as from the manufacturer come from manufacturer specs, manuals and help pages (or major retailer
// listings of them) as of October 2026.  Manufacturers mostly do NOT publish kettle loss or boil-off rate, so those
// are estimates scaled by element power and vessel size: every brewer should calibrate them on their own system.
//
// Modelling choices, which apply to every profile here:
//  • There is no mash_tun, because the mash happens in the kettle (see the description of Equipment in the schema).
//    So the kettle is the only vessel, and grain_absorption_rate and foundation_volume are at the top level.  No hlt
//    (sparge water heating is separate and varies), and no fermentation/aging/packaging vessels (not part of these
//    systems).
//  • default_batch_size is the manufacturer's stated (maximum) batch size, ie volume into the fermenter, where given.
//    Otherwise (Brewferm Beer Brew 25 and the Brewtools "pro" systems) it is the maximum pre-boil volume less a 60
//    minute boil-off and the kettle loss.  We also cap it at that figure where the manufacturer's batch size is bigger
//    (eg because it assumes a shorter boil or lower boil-off than our estimate), so that a recipe with a 60 minute boil
//    does not need more pre-boil wort than the kettle can hold.  (A program reading this file is likely to work out a
//    default pre-boil volume as batch size + 60 minute boil-off + kettle loss.)  Where we have done this, the profile's
//    notes give the manufacturer's figure.
//  • When the malt pipe / basket / bag is lifted, wort below the bottom plate ends up in the boil rather than being
//    lost.  That volume below the plate is the foundation_volume, which is given wherever the manufacturer publishes
//    it (and omitted otherwise).
//  • grain_absorption_rate is 0.8 L/kg (Grainfather's figure; ~0.1 gal/lb for the Anvil profiles), which is typical
//    for an unsqueezed malt pipe.
//  • The kettle's max_volume is the maximum usable fill / pre-boil volume where known, otherwise the nominal capacity.
//  • weight and specific_heat_capacity (304 stainless, 500 J/kg·K) are given only where the empty weight is known.
//  • internal_diameter = opening_diameter (open-topped cylinder); given only where a diameter is known, and
//    approximate (derived from outside dimensions).
//======================================================================================================================
